  "raw_label": "appellant_lost",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Devender Kumar Goyal vs Rakesh Kumar Jain on 3 October, 2024 Author: Vikas Bahl Bench: Vikas Bahl Neutral Citation No:=2024:PHHC:131756 Cr-5795-2024 1 In The High Court Of Punjab & Haryana At Chandigarh *** Cr-5795-2024 Date of decision : 03.10.2024 Devender Kumar Goyal ... Petitioner Versus Rakesh Kumar Jain ... Respondent Coram: Hon'Ble Mr. Justice Vikas Bahl",
    "facts": "3. The trial Court vide order dated 20.09.2024 had permitted the petitioner to place on record the certified copy of the reply / written statement in the case titled as \"Rakesh Kumar vs. Subhash Chand\" by observing that the copy of the petition and other documents related to the said case had already been filed by the present petitioner and considering it to be an inadvertent mistake, the production of the said reply / written statement, was allowed, but however, with respect to the complaint, which was sought to be produced in additional evidence, the trial Court had observed that the same was not relevant for the purpose of adjudication of the case and there was no requirement to produce the copy of the said 2 of 5 Neutral Citation No:=2024:PHHC:131756 complaint and the related documents. 4.",
    "arguments": "Learned counsel for the petitioner has submitted that the complaint is necessary for adjudication of the present case inasmuch as it is the plea of the respondent-landlord in the eviction petition, moreso, in paragraph 4 (ii) that he was working in his brother's shop which was in the name and style of M/s Jain Mishthan Bhandaar and it was Subhash Chand Jain, who was the proprietor of M/s Jain Misthan Bhandar and the said document (Annexure P-3) which is the complaint dated 03.09.2018 would show that the present respondent has been shown as a food business operator which would infer that the said shop is under the ownership of the respondent-landlord. 2 Learned counsel for the petitioner has not been able to refer any provision either in the Code of Civil Procedure or in the Rent Act to show that a party has the right to lead additional evidence."
  },
